WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.550
I have actually climbed ice
with a steak knife once.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:09.630
I did it as a joke,

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.880
but there are already
holes in there and stuff,

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.400
so, no, that dagger would not work.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.060
My name is Will Gadd, and I'm
a professional ice climber,

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.610
Red Bull athlete from Canmore, Alberta,

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:23.550
and I've been ice climbing
for almost 40 years.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.040
Today, we're going to be
looking at ice-climbing scenes

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.463
in movies and judge how real they are.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.028
You wouldn't be climbing
together like this

00:00:37.028 --> 00:00:39.219
because if one person falls off,

00:00:39.219 --> 00:00:41.344
you're kind of all going to die.

00:00:41.344 --> 00:00:43.170
Simul climbing is a tactic you use

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:44.790
on relatively easy terrain,

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.280
like, maybe 50-, 60-degree terrain,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.000
and it's just in case something
catastrophic goes wrong.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.200
It's high risk.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.230
It's not something you
generally want to do,

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.330
and you definitely don't want to do it

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:01.747
when you're scaling the side
of this immense piece of ice.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:11.670
You get cracks like that

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.860
that go really frightening distances.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.650
I've had it fracture,
like, 30 feet horizontally.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.950
So it's a bit exaggerated,
but not that much, actually.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.870
I saw that scene and I was like, ah!

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.060
But most ice climbers don't
climb in terrain like that.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.920
You kind of have to be weird to go there.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.840
OK, it's totally unrealistic
in every other way,

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.748
but at least that part was kind of real.

00:01:34.424 --> 00:01:36.180
Oh! Stop, stop, stop.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.850
OK, so, that rope does not stretch.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:43.170
They would be cut in half,
worst-case scenario, by the rope,

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:46.710
or they would break every
major organ in their bodies.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:47.700
It's like a steel cable.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.320
Imagine hucking yourself off a building

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.430
tied to a steel cable.

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:52.110
It's just not going to go well.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.870
So, modern climbing ropes
are basically bungees,

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:58.620
and they elongate and
absorb the impact slowly,

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.410
specifically so you don't
break your internal organs.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.270
This is not a survivable situation,

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.210
and that's just assuming
the person up top there

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.400
could actually hold the
impact, which they can't.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.413
They would get yanked off so fast.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.520
When you're hanging on a rope in space,

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.580
it's really hard to generate
lateral momentum like this.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.280
Pretty much impossible. I've
tried it a lot of times.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.290
So it doesn't matter how
much you jerk and twist,

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.000
you just sort of end up going in a circle

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.050
on the end of the rope.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.587
It's one. This is totally unrealistic

00:02:30.587 --> 00:02:33.161
and never gonna happen.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.530
As a mountaineer, when you self-arrest,

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.600
you flip onto your stomach,
you dig your toes in,

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.510
which buddy doesn't do when
he's sliding down here.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.180
He would've stopped this really fast

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.370
if he'd just dug his toes in right away.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.480
You know, his pick is just
ripping through the snow

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.680
the whole time when he does self-arrest.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.723
Somehow it holds in that situation.

00:03:01.129 --> 00:03:02.070
Hang on!

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:03.930
I always love it in scenes like this

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:05.580
when they say "hang on."

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.320
Like, what are they gonna do, let go?

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.550
It's super sunny out.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.890
Why are her sunglasses on her head?

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.650
She would be snow-blind
in, like, 10 seconds.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.110
There are so many things
that are unbelievable,

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:17.880
but that's almost the
most unbelievable thing.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.710
You'd definitely have your sunglasses

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:20.985
over your eyes at this point.

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:31.320
And when you break a cornice
like that, it breaks.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:34.720
When you get a fracture in
snow like that, it goes.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.200
You don't get kind of a
second chance, unfortunately.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.060
And having been in that
situation where it broke,

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.615
I ran the other way at speed.

00:03:41.615 --> 00:03:43.140
You're like, "Get away from there."

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.007
And she's like, "I'll
just keep standing here!"

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.683
Not really what you
want to be doing there.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:56.760
OK, so that's not going to work.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:58.880
You're not going to put your pick

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.200
gently into the snow and
then have it hold anything.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.960
It's just going to rip through like crazy.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.800
Maybe if you pounded
it in with a big hammer

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.435
and it were ice, you'd get, you know,

00:04:11.435 --> 00:04:13.350
a sort of OK emergency anchor, but no.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:14.823
That's not gonna work.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:21.450
I've done things like that,

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.840
jumping from one desert tower,
for example, to another.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:27.990
You can do things sort of like that.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.990
What you can't do is fall,
like, what looks like,

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:33.450
let's use the most
conservative number we can,

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:35.800
30 feet vertically

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.658
and stick your ice tools into rock

00:04:38.658 --> 00:04:41.908
and then not completely blow

00:04:41.908 --> 00:04:44.460
both your arms out of the sockets.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.020
Say you can hang on to the ice tools.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:47.880
Like, let's say that
somehow in your forearms

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.310
you got the juice, you can
hang on to those ice tools.

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:54.210
I keep picturing this
set of arms on the wall

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:57.240
while his body is somewhere
thousands of feet below.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:59.040
Like, that's what would happen.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.875
You would just rip everything out.

00:05:00.875 --> 00:05:02.025
It'd be like, pew!

00:05:02.025 --> 00:05:03.091
It's in the negatives.

00:05:03.091 --> 00:05:04.980
Like, if the other ones are one or twos,

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.473
there's not enough room
in that scale to go low.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:13.460
Galadriel: And the
threat at last was ended.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.840
This terrain is really cool.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.760
So if anybody knows where
this is and it's real,

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:20.252
please send me an email. I'd
like to have a go at that.

00:05:23.627 --> 00:05:25.860
Galadriel: I wish I could be one of them.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:27.720
She seems very nice,
but she is going to die.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.480
That dagger is not going to
go into the ice like that.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.760
It could be kind of like
trying to split firewood

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.790
by pushing the axe-head into
the wood with your hands.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.730
You gotta swing it. And there's
no force in that dagger.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:40.650
You just can't move fast enough.

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:43.620
This is designed to swing
really fast into ice.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.983
It's got a razor-sharp point on it.

00:05:46.983 --> 00:05:47.883
It's a weapon.

00:05:47.883 --> 00:05:50.070
You want this, not the ice-dagger scene.

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.380
That is just flat-out not going to work.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:54.060
You certainly couldn't jump laterally,

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:55.740
stick it in, and not die.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:58.440
All this metal, that's gotta be so cold!

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:00.480
She's got, like, chain mail on her head.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.038
She's going to get frostbite from that.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:06.720
OK, where's, what are they hanging on to?

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:07.890
Hang on, stop for a second.

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:10.740
But that person has their hand on the ice,

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.930
and then look where the
left ice tool is there.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:16.620
It's just dangling. Like,
that is not the way forward.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:18.240
You want to have that in your hand.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:20.220
You're not just going
to latch onto the ice.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.460
You can climb ice without an ice tool,

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:25.980
but if you have one, you're
sure gonna want to use it.

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:28.500
But I just loved how the ice
tool is just dangling there.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:30.100
It's like, "Ah, we don't need ice tools."

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:36.240
That's something that's
a really big hazard

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:37.500
in ice climbing, is you have to think

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.320
about where the ice you
knock off is going to go.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:42.270
That's, like, ice-climbing 101.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:44.700
I have seen this happen in real life.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:47.370
It's usually involving ice
climbers from warm places.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:51.180
You gotta put the belays out
of the way of falling ice.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:52.920
It's really important.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:54.303
And then they get hit
in the face with ice,

00:06:54.303 --> 00:06:55.286
and they're like, "Oh, mate,

00:06:55.286 --> 00:06:57.377
you hit me in the face with ice!"

00:06:57.377 --> 00:06:59.749
It's like, don't stand in
the way of the falling ice.

00:06:59.749 --> 00:07:02.070
And then in terms of the
technology or believability,

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:05.433
yeah, we're down in the
one, two kind of range here.

00:07:15.102 --> 00:07:16.500
So, I really like this one,

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:18.300
because this one actually happened.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:19.890
BASE jumpers have hucked themselves

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:22.950
and gotten stuck on cliffs regularly.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:25.155
You know, my friends on
the search-and-rescue crew

00:07:25.155 --> 00:07:26.850
here where I live and
then in other places,

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:30.540
they deal with this not,
you know, it's not never.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:32.100
It's, like, a couple times a season,

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:33.903
somebody's going to do this somewhere.

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:43.230
This has gotta hurt your foot.

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:44.310
Like, there's a knife in there.

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:46.368
I can't see this going
well, kicking the ice.

00:07:46.368 --> 00:07:47.884
This is not a good idea.

00:07:47.884 --> 00:07:50.520
But yeah, and I have actually climbed ice

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:53.220
with a steak knife once.
I did it as a joke,

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:55.470
but there are already
holes in there and stuff,

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:57.933
so, no, that dagger would not work.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:04.467
You need about 2 centimeters
or an inch of ice

00:08:04.467 --> 00:08:08.017
to climb it in a vertical
setting if you're really good.

00:08:08.017 --> 00:08:11.010
I'm pretty sure with,
like, throwing knives,

00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:13.320
you'd probably need a bit more than that.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:17.400
That ice looks more like almost
what we would call verglas,

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.370
or just very thin ice.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:23.370
And, yeah, that's not gonna work.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:25.278
But I love the creativity.

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:33.450
I've actually had this happen!

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:35.790
Sheep do show up in climbing areas,

00:08:35.790 --> 00:08:36.930
and they're a real hazard

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:39.120
because they knock rocks off on you.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.160
I actually almost got killed this way.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:44.400
This herd of sheep showed
up at the top of the cliff,

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:45.810
and they're running around up there,

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:47.370
and they're knocking rocks off.

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:49.170
And we all had to run into the cliff.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:51.120
The sheep don't move like
this in this terrain.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:52.980
They don't have crampons on their feet.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:54.240
They're not moving like this.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.432
They're really good, but
they're not that good.

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:02.430
OK, hold up, hold up.

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:04.950
What he's just found there is actually

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:07.855
one of the biggest hazards

00:09:07.855 --> 00:09:09.870
that you can't see in ice climbing.

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:11.850
You kind of have to develop a feel for it,

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:13.800
and it's called an onion skin.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:16.710
And what happens is you get
snow on a ledge like that,

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:19.783
and then the water from the
ice climb keeps falling on top,

00:09:19.783 --> 00:09:21.200
and it makes a new layer of ice.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:25.560
So it's got snow underneath,
and it's got ice on top of it,

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:28.320
and it'll make layers, just like that.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:29.790
And this is a little overdone.

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:33.300
Ice doesn't just fail like this.
But it's sort of plausible.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:35.130
I actually had something
like this kind of happen,

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:36.630
where an onion skin broke,

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:38.610
and then kind of wound up running in place

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:41.910
as this onion skin shot
out from underneath me.

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:45.690
So, it's not exactly like this, but it,

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:47.580
you know, kind of realistic.

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:50.040
Ice-climbing instruction level was poor.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:51.286
I'm gonna go three.

00:09:56.669 --> 00:09:59.400
You can tell by their
movements these are people

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.860
that have actually ice climbed.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:04.380
I think one of them is my
friend Barry Blanchard.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:05.730
It's pretty old school,

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:07.530
and they're hamming it
up hard for the camera.

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:09.690
Legs are out like this to the side.

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:11.550
You don't normally ice climb like that,

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:12.840
but it looks good on television.

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:16.110
I have done that exact move
more than once on camera,

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:17.857
where I can hear the director yelling,

00:10:17.857 --> 00:10:20.700
"Get more limb separation!"
to them, you know?

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:22.320
That's the classic line.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:24.308
But their feet aren't blowing.
Their feet are generally

00:10:24.308 --> 00:10:27.102
about shoulder-width
apart and underneath them.

00:10:27.102 --> 00:10:28.050
It's like in any sport.

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:29.760
When your feet go way out to the sides,

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:31.233
bad things are going to happen.

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:39.270
So, I think in this scene,

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:42.907
they're training for the
ice-climbing part of climbing K2.

00:10:42.907 --> 00:10:45.930
Mountaineering is about getting
to the top of mountains,

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:48.990
but you gotta have skills
on rock and on ice,

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:51.780
different sort of mediums
that you travel on.

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:54.570
But in terms of the
technique and tactic to use,

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:56.730
this is all pretty realistic.

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:59.173
You might not be doing
this form of steep climbing

00:10:59.173 --> 00:11:02.583
on K2, but you'd be doing
something similar to it.

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:12.870
The whole camera is tilted
to make it look steeper.

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:14.130
And you can tell

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:17.460
'cause you can see the
pack straps hanging down.

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:19.013
They're not hanging down the right way.

00:11:19.013 --> 00:11:21.488
They're hanging down toward
the ground on this one.

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:25.650
They're kind of using weird combinations,

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:28.020
and so they've got a
technical tool in one hand

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:30.630
and a longer mountaineering
ice axe in the other.

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:34.453
And the technical
ice-climbing tools tend to be

00:11:34.453 --> 00:11:38.370
very organized for the
pick to go into ice well,

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:41.280
whereas the mountaineer
axes tend to be longer

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:45.030
and more focused on
snow and softer mediums,

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:47.490
generally speaking, than hard-water ice.

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:48.990
But it's just tools of the trade,

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:52.260
and they're all meant for ice and snow.

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:53.340
It's not a huge stretch.

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:55.230
If I had to rate it, I'd
give it, like, a nine.

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:56.523
It's pretty accurate.

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:03.120
That is in France, and it's
one of the coolest ice climbs

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:05.220
in the world, so they
chose a good ice climb,

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:08.043
but it's definitely not the
Lho La icefall in Nepal.

00:12:09.597 --> 00:12:11.197
[phone rings]

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:13.373
Hello?

00:12:13.373 --> 00:12:16.098
Phone: Ms. Woods. Pleasure
to make your acquaintance.

00:12:16.098 --> 00:12:18.810
That is just anatomically
not where you would stop

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:21.960
to take a phone call, like, ever.

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:23.070
It just makes no sense.

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:25.350
You'd be either pulled
into the ice or swinging,

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:27.540
and her tool is junk.

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:31.680
Like, if you're soloing, that
is not an acceptable tool.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:34.830
Most ice climbers will solo
to some degree or another,

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:36.540
meaning not use a rope.

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:40.530
The difference between soloing
and using a rope on ice

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:43.260
is a lot less than the difference between

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:45.990
using a rope and not on rock.

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:49.260
Ice climbing, the goal is very,
very much not to fall off,

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:50.130
even with a rope,

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:52.230
because you've got all
these sharp things on you,

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:53.220
and if you fall off,

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:56.070
they tend to catch and either impale you

00:12:56.070 --> 00:12:58.560
or break your legs or flip you upside down

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:01.337
and then you break your
neck or your head, so,

00:13:01.337 --> 00:13:02.970
you don't fall off in ice climbing

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.710
is kind of the general rule,

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:06.844
but you want to use much better technique

00:13:06.844 --> 00:13:10.143
and tactics than is going
on here. This is sketchy.

